something similar to radar looking through fog.

37
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:58,460
Despite centuries of technological progress, fire remains one of civilization's biggest problems.

38
00:03:00,940 --> 00:03:07,780
It takes 75,000 fire trucks and more than a million firefighters to protect America's cities.

39
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:16,860
It's a veritable army, fighting a battle that never ends in a war as old as history itself.

40
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:35,180
For thousands of years, fire was a magical and mysterious force.

41
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,060
Humans are the only species to ever attempt to control it.

42
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:45,380
Fire has always been a friend and foe of civilization.

43
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:52,500
It served our needs in terms of keeping us warm and cooking our food and doing many other important tasks.

44
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:57,880
However, it can turn around and be our enemies just as quickly, and that's been the case throughout history as well.

45
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:08,640
It wasn't until the 1700s that an inquisitive French chemist, Antoine Lavoisier, unlocked fire's secret.

46
00:04:08,641 --> 00:04:15,640
With simple experiments, Lavoisier discovered that fire has a three-part chemical reaction.

47
00:04:15,641 --> 00:04:20,640
It starts when a substance like wood is heated.

48
00:04:20,641 --> 00:04:25,640
The wood itself does not ignite.

49
00:04:25,641 --> 00:04:30,640
Instead it releases combustible gas which combines with oxygen and causes a flame.

50
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:31,726
a flame.

51
00:04:31,727 --> 00:04:42,720
LaVosier's research led to the basic scientific principle, the fire triangle. The

52
00:04:42,721 --> 00:04:48,060
three sides of the triangle are the three things necessary for fire to occur, heat,

53
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:53,400
fuel, and oxygen. Remove any one of the three and the flames go out.

54
00:04:53,401 --> 00:05:03,780
For centuries, firefighters have relied on the triangle concept.

55
00:05:03,781 --> 00:05:08,680
When they spray a burning building with water, they're attacking one side of the triangle,

56
00:05:08,681 --> 00:05:13,880
removing heat that a fire needs.

57
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:18,680
Fire extinguishers attack a different side of the triangle.

58
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:23,240
They snuff out the fire with chemicals that prevent oxygen from reaching the flames.

59
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:34,920
When battling a brush fire, crews attack the third side of the triangle by removing the fuel that a fire needs to advance.

60
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:48,921
ancient civilizations didn't need to know how fireworks to appreciate its destructive potential

61
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:57,720
the world's first fire department was organized in 23 BC by the Roman Emperor Augustus

62
00:05:57,721 --> 00:06:04,360
The Romans invented history's first firefighting device

63
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:09,320
A wooden syringe that sucked water from buckets and squirted it toward the flames

64
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:15,760
Rome was also protected by a private fire force

65
00:06:15,761 --> 00:06:18,480
Owned by an enterprising construction contractor

66
00:06:18,481 --> 00:06:24,140
Marcus Crassus would rush his men to a fire

67
00:06:24,141 --> 00:06:28,180
and negotiate a fee with the homeowner before attacking the blaze.

68
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:34,840
Crassus wound up owning nearly every building he saved,

69
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:37,720
becoming Rome's largest landlord.

70
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,420
Early America was built to burn.

71
00:06:53,340 --> 00:06:56,840
Homes were constructed with thatched roofs with wooden shingles.

72
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:02,200
A spark from a chimney could start a blaze that threatened an entire village.

73
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:09,280
When flames were spotted, the cry, throw out your buckets, would echo through the town.

74
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:15,800
People scrambled to form a bucket brigade to the nearest source of water.

75
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:21,020
Everyone in the community is expected to participate in the bucket brigade.

76
00:07:21,021 --> 00:07:27,840
The common sense of good with the bucket brigades was that you would have to help your neighbor because the next day they would be helping you.

77
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:37,360
For a speedy response, every homeowner was required to keep three leather buckets filled with water.

78
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:44,020
Civic leaders like Benjamin Franklin and George Washington would coordinate the fire attack.

79
00:07:44,021 --> 00:07:51,780
an organized bucket brigade was essential for a village to survive

80
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:57,200
it was also an important hub of social life

81
00:07:57,201 --> 00:08:03,860
you would have the men on the side of the brigade that were moving the full buckets towards the fire

82
00:08:03,861 --> 00:08:08,320
and the women would be on the side returning the empty buckets to the water source

83
00:08:08,321 --> 00:08:13,300
and it would sometimes occur to some of the mothers

84
00:08:13,301 --> 00:08:16,160
that it would be a good idea to put their unwed daughters

85
00:08:16,161 --> 00:08:19,060
across from the most eligible bachelors in the bucket brigade.

86
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:23,400
Even the best bucket brigade had limited success.

87
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:28,380
Often all a brigade could do was fight spot fires on adjacent buildings

88
00:08:28,381 --> 00:08:30,480
to prevent the whole village from burning.

89
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:36,240
It normally wasn't the building that was burning that they tried to extinguish.

90
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:37,860
It was the surrounding houses.

91
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:43,900
For usually, many times, these buildings that were burning were so far gone that they couldn't be saved.

92
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:49,140
What Americans needed was better firefighting equipment.

93
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:51,800
Soon, they would get it.

94
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:55,480
A technological revolution was coming.

95
00:08:55,481 --> 00:09:09,481
The need to conquer fire has inspired hundreds of inventions.

96
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:16,200
But one device developed in the 1700s revolutionized firefighting.

97
00:09:16,201 --> 00:09:18,460
The piston pumper engine.

98
00:09:18,461 --> 00:09:29,220
Pistons provided enough mechanical force to suck water from a well or stream and shoot it toward a fire

99
00:09:29,221 --> 00:09:37,720
Early piston pumpers were hauled by hand and powered by crews of up to 60 men

100
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:43,840
Long wooden rails were attached to the piston pump

101
00:09:43,841 --> 00:09:50,840
Raising and lowering the rails was a back-breaking chore that required remarkable teamwork.

102
00:09:50,841 --> 00:09:59,840
During that time, there were chants and there were men encouraging each other why this process would be going on.

103
00:09:59,841 --> 00:10:04,840
And don't give up. We're putting the fire out. We're saving the life.

104
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:15,520
The piston engine allowed firefighters to work from a safe distance, up to 200 feet away, putting a steady stream of water exactly where it was needed.

105
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:26,020
Steam engines replaced hand pumps in the mid-1800s.

106
00:10:26,021 --> 00:10:35,780
a steamer was powered by coal and worked like a railroad locomotive

107
00:10:35,781 --> 00:10:39,900
the steam drove a powerful piston pump

108
00:10:39,901 --> 00:10:47,380
a steamer could deliver more than 800 gallons of water per minute

109
00:10:47,381 --> 00:10:49,940
and required only two men to operate

110
00:10:49,941 --> 00:10:58,360
The advantage of steam is no longer do you have to have 30 men laboring just to pump water.

111
00:10:58,960 --> 00:11:03,600
Now we're using steam to drive the pistons to drive the pump and pump the water.

112
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:08,860
Now the men, the firefighters, can be used at the front of the fire to actually fight the fire and save lives.

113
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:16,720
The age of steam was the most dramatic era in firefighting history.

114
