and then the dispatcher would look up in the book here

141
00:13:58,101 --> 00:14:00,320
and see who was supposed to go

142
00:14:00,321 --> 00:14:02,440
and what type of a fire they may be involved in.

143
00:14:06,340 --> 00:14:09,880
The firehouse sprang to action when an alarm came in.

144
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:12,660
Everything was organized for speed.

145
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:30,960
At the scene, specialized crews called companies would perform different tasks.

146
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:36,720
Engine companies would lay hoses and prepare the steamer.

147
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:39,980
Ladder companies would set up the aerial equipment.

148
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:47,840
By 1870, ladders could reach up to 75 feet,

149
00:14:47,841 --> 00:14:53,380
although they were difficult to place because they had to be extended with crude hand cranks.

150
00:14:54,700 --> 00:15:03,760
Still ladders were the ultimate life-saving device, allowing firefighters to cheat death by plucking victims from peril.

151
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:11,760
Images of dramatic rescues boosted public confidence in America's fire departments.

152
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:19,940
Many people felt the professional firefighter with his elaborate equipment reduce the risk of death.

153
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:26,453
Firefighters knew better.

154
00:15:26,454 --> 00:15:30,940
American cities were growing faster than their fire departments.

155
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,200
Warehouse and tenement districts were disasters waiting to happen.

156
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:39,520
In many instances, these buildings were connected.

157
00:15:39,860 --> 00:15:43,600
They were rows of buildings, warehouses and townhouses.

158
00:15:43,601 --> 00:15:50,300
And the concept of not only compacted urban areas, but tall urban areas,

159
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:56,080
meant that they had to actually have new and specialized techniques to fight the fire.

160
00:15:56,680 --> 00:15:59,320
These lessons were learned the hard way.

161
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:07,920
In 1871 and 1906, two of America's largest cities would burn to the ground.

162
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,280
Hi, I'm John Travolta from the new movie Ladder 49.

163
00:16:12,281 --> 00:16:15,220
And you're watching Modern Marvels on the History Channel.

164
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:23,640
By 1871, Chicago was America's fastest growing city,

165
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:30,100
with a fire department comprised of 16 steam engines and 219 men.

166
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:33,860
Chicago's fire chief wanted more,

167
00:16:34,260 --> 00:16:38,120
insisting his force wasn't big enough to protect a city built of wood.

168
00:16:38,121 --> 00:16:45,320
Two-thirds of Chicago's 60,000 buildings were made with wood.

169
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:49,940
Even the streets and sidewalks were paved with wood blocks.

170
00:16:52,100 --> 00:16:56,180
Chicago officials rejected the chief's request for more men and equipment.

171
00:16:57,460 --> 00:17:00,220
The decision would lead to disaster.

172
00:17:00,221 --> 00:17:02,090
At 9.30 p.m.

173
00:17:02,091 --> 00:17:15,800
on October 8, 1871, flames broke out in a barn on Decoven Street on Chicago's west side.

174
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:23,220
The fire department, which was overworked and stretched dangerously thin, was slow to respond.

175
00:17:23,221 --> 00:17:30,780
By the time the men were in place at the scene, the blaze was spreading out of control.

176
00:17:32,460 --> 00:17:40,640
Stiff, autumn winds were whipping the fire like a bellows, creating tornadoes of flame that ripped through the heart of the city.

177
00:17:45,960 --> 00:17:50,060
In two hours, 500 buildings were ablaze.

178
00:17:50,061 --> 00:17:57,240
it's massive they're sending up millions of embers that are raining down in other parts

179
00:17:57,241 --> 00:18:02,120
of the city starting many many spot fires is a wind of fire that's approaching

180
00:18:02,121 --> 00:18:10,720
firemen tried to make strategic stands at major intersections but their steam-driven pumpers

181
00:18:10,721 --> 00:18:15,600
were no match for the firestorm there was very good equipment available at the time for fighting

182
00:18:15,601 --> 00:18:17,690
fires but it still wasn't good enough.

183
00:18:17,691 --> 00:18:20,220
The firefighters were able to get water to the

184
00:18:20,221 --> 00:18:25,360
seat of a fire but not enough, not in sufficient quantities yet.

185
00:18:25,361 --> 00:18:29,100
It's everything the fire department can do to stop it at a street or stop it at some

186
00:18:29,101 --> 00:18:34,240
kind of natural fire break and even then those fires would simply hop right over those streets

187
00:18:34,241 --> 00:18:41,880
and just continue to burn and burn and flatten everything in their path.

188
00:18:41,881 --> 00:18:46,600
At the courthouse, the mayor was desperately sending telegrams to nearby cities.

189
00:18:48,300 --> 00:18:53,820
Chicago is in flames. Send your entire department to help us.

190
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:56,140
Mayor Roswell Mason.

191
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:02,980
For 30 hours, Chicago was gripped by panic.

192
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:09,860
The dogs of hell were upon the housetops, bounding from one to another.

193
00:19:09,861 --> 00:19:12,860
The fire was moving like ocean surf.

194
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:15,500
Horace White, eyewitness.

195
00:19:16,540 --> 00:19:20,940
The whole air was filled with falling cinders.

196
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:24,440
It looked like a snowstorm lit by colored fire.

197
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:27,540
Alexander Freer, eyewitness.

198
00:19:30,260 --> 00:19:35,100
It took a major rainstorm to finally end the great Chicago fire.

199
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:40,320
300 people were dead, and a third of the city was in ashes.

200
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:44,740
100,000 residents were homeless.

201
00:19:46,240 --> 00:19:52,300
The disaster prompted cities throughout the nation to pour resources into fire preparedness.

202
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:59,620
They purchased more equipment and required the use of fireproof building materials.

203
00:19:59,621 --> 00:20:06,340
However, one city, San Francisco, failed to follow the trend

204
00:20:06,341 --> 00:20:18,660
San Francisco, 1906

205
00:20:18,661 --> 00:20:22,680
A bustling boom town with a major problem

206
00:20:22,681 --> 00:20:24,020
Water

207
00:20:24,021 --> 00:20:30,020
Fire officials were worried about the city's aging water mains.

208
00:20:30,021 --> 00:20:36,020
They proposed the construction of an emergency backup system which could pump salt water from the ocean.

209
00:20:36,021 --> 00:20:41,020
Here is a city surrounded by water on three sides.

210
00:20:41,021 --> 00:20:42,962
However, they need to have that readily available.

211
00:20:42,963 --> 00:20:45,020
They need it up in the streets where they can use it.

212
00:20:45,021 --> 00:20:51,020
They need it in the sense that it's not going to be compromised during a big fire or an earthquake or any other event.

213
00:20:54,020 --> 00:21:06,280
Disaster struck San Francisco at 5.13 a.m. on April 18, 1906.

214
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:11,520
An earthquake measuring 8.25 on the Richter scale.

215
00:21:15,780 --> 00:21:20,280
As buildings crumbled, lanterns and gas lamps set them ablaze.

216
00:21:21,060 --> 00:21:23,740
In an instant, there were flames throughout the city.

217
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:38,020
Firemen responded quickly, but their greatest fear had come true.

218
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:44,980
All of the water mains were severed, and so the fire department had no water to fight these fires.

219
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:51,060
There were fires cropping up that if they had water available, the chief felt they could easily fight.

220
00:21:51,061 --> 00:21:54,120
They probably would have had a fighting chance of putting these fires out.

221
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:58,380
But with no water, all they could do was helplessly sit there and watch the fire spread.

222
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:07,180
San Francisco hadn't built the emergency system that the fire department requested.

223
00:22:07,940 --> 00:22:11,500
As a result, the city now looked like Armageddon.

224
00:22:11,501 --> 00:22:24,920
The hopeless situation forced Assistant Fire Chief John Leatherlungs Doherty to take drastic action

225
00:22:24,921 --> 00:22:29,720
Demolish part of the city to create a line of flattened rubble

226
00:22:29,721 --> 00:22:35,360
Since rubble catches fire less easily than standing buildings

227
00:22:35,361 --> 00:22:38,300
The chief hoped he could stop the fire from spreading

228
00:22:38,301 --> 00:22:46,440
the military was called from the presidio and they with cannons and whatever else was available

229
00:22:46,441 --> 00:22:52,000
destroyed the buildings they blew them up along van ness avenue to create that fire break

230
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:05,000
it was very much a gutsy thing to take it and start destroying people's buildings for the good

231
00:23:05,001 --> 00:23:05,959
of everyone else.

232
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:09,740
You had to write off some buildings and say, we're going to have to

233
00:23:09,741 --> 00:23:12,320
destroy these by blowing them up and save the city.

234
00:23:15,780 --> 00:23:18,325
Doherty's desperate decision paid off.

235
00:23:18,326 --> 00:23:21,540
The firebreak was strategically placed along the

236
00:23:21,541 --> 00:23:26,847
Juan Boulevard, where water hydrants were still working.

237
00:23:26,848 --> 00:23:29,500
A curtain of water along Van

238
00:23:29,501 --> 00:23:35,580
S Avenue delayed the fire's advance until rain began to fall and finally extinguished

239
00:23:35,581 --> 00:23:36,300
the blaze.

240
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:50,260
The San Francisco earthquake fire burned for three days, incinerating 514 blocks and claiming

241
00:23:50,261 --> 00:23:52,360
674 lives.

242
00:23:52,361 --> 00:23:56,500
It remains the costliest fire in U.S. history.

243
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:02,800
$350 million in damage, the equivalent of $6 billion today.

244
00:24:06,440 --> 00:24:11,340
San Francisco was the last of America's citywide conflagrations.

245
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:19,860
The scope of the disaster prompted cities across the country to upgrade their water systems to fight fire.

246
00:24:22,360 --> 00:24:30,220
As well, a second revolution in technology was coming that would dramatically help firefighters do their job.

247
00:24:36,460 --> 00:24:41,680
The early 1900s brought a new efficiency to America's fire departments.

248
00:24:41,681 --> 00:24:54,240
Motorized fire trucks often made the difference between life and death.

249
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:14,340
Motorized equipment began to appear in 1912.

250
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:19,960
Some of the earliest rigs were simple tractors that merely replaced a team of horses.

251
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:29,000
Other units were made from modified Model T automobiles.

252
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:38,720
Engineers then developed a new concept, the fire truck,

253
00:25:38,721 --> 00:25:47,740
a specialized unit that all at once could haul men, hoses, ladders, and a tank of water for immediate use.

254
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:53,460
Fire chiefs bought the new engines by the thousands.

255
00:25:54,240 --> 00:26:02,980
Motorized apparatus offered some great advantages because it allowed firefighters to get their equipment to fires much more quickly,

256
00:26:02,981 --> 00:26:09,740
get more equipment, bigger equipment, faster equipment, get all that to the scene of the fire where they really needed it.

257
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:17,100
The motorized fire engine transformed America's fire departments in less than 10 years.

258
00:26:17,960 --> 00:26:21,600
They dramatically reduced the time it took to respond,

259
00:26:21,601 --> 00:26:27,040
giving firefighters a better chance of keeping a small fire from growing.

260
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:34,420
The engine's powerful pumps could supply more than a thousand gallons of water every minute

261
00:26:34,421 --> 00:26:38,680
and keep the water pressurized through hundreds of feet of hose.

262
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:47,160
The improved water supply allowed firefighters for the first time

263
00:26:47,161 --> 00:26:50,680
to take their attack inside a burning structure.

264
00:26:51,600 --> 00:27:01,360
By being able to go into a building, firefighters now have changed the whole focus of firefighting

265
00:27:01,361 --> 00:27:06,320
from preventing the spread from building to building to controlling a fire within a building

266
00:27:06,321 --> 00:27:10,020
and even save you an occupant that may be trapped in the building.

267
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:15,020
Ladders were greatly improved in the 1920s and 30s.

268
00:27:15,021 --> 00:27:22,020
Ladder trucks grew so long they required a second driver in the back to control the rear wheels around corners.

269
00:27:22,021 --> 00:27:32,020
Wooden ladders gave way to lightweight metal and the old hand cranks were replaced by motors.

270
00:27:32,021 --> 00:27:38,020
A ladder company could raise and position its equipment in moments.

271
00:27:59,754 --> 00:28:06,020
Now, firefighters can enter a building on different floors and spray water on the blaze from above.

272
00:28:06,021 --> 00:28:11,460
use of motorized equipment allowed firefighters to take a more aggressive approach.

273
00:28:16,320 --> 00:28:23,800
However, this presented a new technological challenge, protecting the firefighters themselves.

274
00:28:36,020 --> 00:28:42,460
For most of U.S. history, artists have portrayed firefighters in an idealized light.

275
00:28:44,940 --> 00:28:51,720
These romantic polychromatic prints give a muted impression of what firefighting was really about.

276
00:28:52,920 --> 00:28:58,500
Chromolithographs showing them, bringing women down ladders and children to safety,

277
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:02,320
were very saleable items in the 19th century,

278
00:29:02,321 --> 00:29:06,520
but it also didn't show the magnitude of the fires that they were combating.

279
00:29:07,580 --> 00:29:13,560
The reality of firefighting was brutal, leaving many men singed and blistered.

280
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:20,780
Until recent times, it was rare when a major blaze didn't claim at least one firefighter's life.

281
00:29:20,781 --> 00:29:34,140
even into the 1950s safety equipment was often crude and ineffective when i went on the fire

282
00:29:34,141 --> 00:29:39,240
department all they issued you was a raincoat and a pair of short boots and uh you couldn't

283
00:29:39,241 --> 00:29:45,960
get into certain places for uh being burned yourself or not enough protective clothing on your body

284
00:29:45,961 --> 00:29:59,720
Safety took a big step forward in 1964 with the development of SCBA, self-contained breathing apparatus.

285
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:11,920
An SCBA unit works like scuba diving equipment delivering fresh air from a fiberglass tank or bottle.

286
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:29,960
The mask protects my face and keeps the fresh air that I'm breathing in from mixing with the toxic gases that I'm exposed to.

287
00:30:30,520 --> 00:30:36,080
I've got a regulator that then reduces the pressure from the bottle down to something that I can breathe.

288
00:30:36,081 --> 00:30:50,081
The need for SCBA became clear as firefighters discovered how a blaze behaves inside a building.

289
00:30:51,700 --> 00:30:57,740
This demonstration tape illustrates how quickly a house can fill with deadly smoke.

290
00:31:00,040 --> 00:31:04,160
A mere chair fire can blacken a room in 90 seconds.

291
00:31:25,880 --> 00:31:27,080
SCBA

292
00:31:27,081 --> 00:31:32,180
SCBA gear allows firefighters to work safely in this toxic environment,

293
00:31:32,181 --> 00:31:36,240
searching for victims and putting water directly on the flames.

294
00:31:38,660 --> 00:31:43,840
In 1967, another breakthrough improved firefighters' safety.

295
00:31:44,900 --> 00:31:47,320
The development of fire-retarding clothing.

296
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:54,960
A suit made with heat-resistant fabric can withstand temperatures of more than 1,000 degrees.

297
00:31:54,961 --> 00:32:01,960
Fire resistant suits and breathing apparatus have improved firefighters safety.

298
00:32:01,961 --> 00:32:05,960
But there is one drawback.

299
00:32:05,961 --> 00:32:12,960
With the equipment that I'm required to wear today, they have taken away all of my senses.

300
00:32:12,961 --> 00:32:17,960
I don't know whether I'm in trouble or not in trouble, how much heat there is.

301
00:32:17,961 --> 00:32:24,960
Rigorous training is now required to teach firefighters how to spot the warning signs of danger.

302
00:32:24,961 --> 00:32:29,380
One of the biggest risks they face is backdraft.

303
00:32:32,120 --> 00:32:41,640
When a room fills with smoke and combustible gas, but there's not enough oxygen to allow the gases to burn, there's the potential for backdraft.

304
00:32:41,641 --> 00:32:50,540
When a firefighter opens a door or window, oxygen rushes in

305
00:32:50,541 --> 00:32:57,700
Flames race toward the incoming oxygen, which is why the phenomenon is called backdraft

306
00:33:01,980 --> 00:33:06,940
A backdraft can cause a house fire to rage out of control in seconds

307
00:33:06,941 --> 00:33:18,400
to prevent backdraft firefighters deliberately poke ventilation holes in the roof

308
00:33:18,401 --> 00:33:21,820
to allow smoke and gases to gradually escape

309
00:33:21,821 --> 00:33:30,280
this is a very misunderstood feature of firefighting that we want to do some damage

310
00:33:30,281 --> 00:33:34,500
and we're going to with ventilating but at the same time we're going to remove all that heat and

311
00:33:34,501 --> 00:33:35,201
smoke.

312
00:33:35,202 --> 00:33:38,880
We're going to make it easier for the firefighters to find the fire and find you

313
00:33:38,881 --> 00:33:39,960
if you're trapped in the building.

314
00:33:42,580 --> 00:33:48,940
The emphasis on safety and rescue were lessons learned from two of history's deadliest fires.

315
00:33:50,100 --> 00:33:56,160
Technology had conquered the specter of citywide conflagrations, but firefighters learned that

316
00:33:56,161 --> 00:34:00,480
disaster could strike a single building with equally deadly results.

317
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:12,500
The newest frontier in firefighting technology is not in the firehouse.

318
00:34:13,520 --> 00:34:19,560
It's in the laboratory, where scientists are studying how building materials react when they burn.

319
00:34:20,940 --> 00:34:26,980
We're starting to be able to simulate how fires would develop in a building on a computer.

320
00:34:26,981 --> 00:34:39,227
And the more sophisticated we can become in predicting how a fire might develop in a building using computer technology and modern science, the more we can get ahead

321
00:34:39,228 --> 00:34:40,340
of the problem.

322
00:34:41,820 --> 00:34:48,660
Engineers use this knowledge to design buildings that can fight a fire even before the fire department arrives.

323
00:34:48,661 --> 00:34:58,000
Structures equipped with automatic sprinkler systems can knock down a fire before it has a chance to grow

324
00:34:58,001 --> 00:35:06,860
Sprinklers were invented in 1874 to protect American factories

325
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:15,860
The valve on a sprinkler head contains a small tube of glass which is filled with a heat sensitive liquid

326
00:35:15,861 --> 00:35:24,160
liquid. As the fire gets hotter and hotter, it heats up this little glass bulb here.

327
00:35:24,920 --> 00:35:30,404
As the fluid gets hotter and hotter, it expands and eventually breaks this glass.

328
00:35:30,405 --> 00:35:31,420
After the glass

329
00:35:31,421 --> 00:35:37,560
is broken, the water is no longer held back and it sprays out onto the fire.

330
00:35:37,561 --> 00:35:46,180
sprinkler heads work independently only the sprinkler directly above the fire goes off

331
00:35:46,181 --> 00:35:52,540
they are remarkably sensitive the heat from a smoldering wastebasket can trigger the

332
00:35:52,541 --> 00:35:54,560
sprinkler into action in less than a minute

333
00:35:54,561 --> 00:36:07,120
a sprinkler doesn't always put a fire out but it dampens the surrounding area to prevent the

334
00:36:07,121 --> 00:36:14,120
flames from spreading until firefighters arrived

335
00:36:15,820 --> 00:36:19,600
although sprinklers have existed for more than a century it took one of

336
00:36:19,601 --> 00:36:26,240
history's most shocking disasters to prompt their widespread use

337
00:36:26,241 --> 00:36:31,140
it happened in nineteen eleven at a new york garment factory

338
00:36:31,141 --> 00:36:34,280
the triangle shirtwaist company

339
00:36:34,281 --> 00:36:38,240
the Triangle Company made a type of blouse known as a shirt waist.

340
00:36:39,880 --> 00:36:43,980
Like other sweatshops of the era, the sewing floor was crowded,

341
00:36:44,760 --> 00:36:46,900
with little in the way of worker protection.

342
00:36:48,420 --> 00:36:53,780
On March 25, 1911, firemen raced to the Triangle Factory

343
00:36:53,781 --> 00:36:57,580
when fabric scraps caught fire on the eighth floor.

344
00:36:57,581 --> 00:37:07,340
Ladders and hoses were unable to reach that high and the blaze quickly spread to the ninth and tenth floors

345
00:37:07,341 --> 00:37:16,180
There was a massive panic, there was a fire escape which quickly became overloaded and collapsed

346
00:37:16,181 --> 00:37:18,780
People plunged to their death in flames

347
00:37:18,781 --> 00:37:26,660
Many of the people on that ninth floor had to choose between either burning to death or jumping and many did both

348
00:37:26,661 --> 00:37:32,900
The building was equipped with buckets and hoses to protect the structure from burning down.

349
00:37:33,720 --> 00:37:36,720
But there was nothing to protect the people inside.

350
00:37:37,900 --> 00:37:42,760
If the upper floors had been equipped with sprinklers, workers might have survived.

351
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:47,400
Instead, scores of employees were dead.

352
00:37:47,401 --> 00:37:59,400
The shocking image of their scorched bodies lined up in a warehouse for family members to identify prompted major reforms.

353
00:38:01,400 --> 00:38:07,040
The Triangle Shirtways fire was a turning point in fire protection history in the United States.

354
00:38:07,520 --> 00:38:13,380
Prior to this fire, there was such an emphasis on keeping whole cities from burning down.

355
00:38:13,381 --> 00:38:19,380
So there was a great emphasis on fire-resistive buildings and fire-resistive construction, property protection.

356
00:38:19,800 --> 00:38:25,500
However, the Triangle Fire clearly delineated the need to protect people.

357
00:38:27,100 --> 00:38:32,360
Government officials responded with reforms that included things we take for granted today.

358
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:41,020
Building codes that mandate the use of sprinklers and cover everything from the design of stairwells to the safety of electrical wiring.

359
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:52,500
However, another fire 30 years later showed that America's fire code still didn't go far enough.

360
00:38:53,880 --> 00:39:01,000
On Thanksgiving weekend 1942, fire suddenly broke out in a crowded Boston nightclub.

361
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:06,880
The coconut grove quickly filled with combustible fumes.

362
00:39:08,200 --> 00:39:11,020
A spark set the mixture ablaze.

363
00:39:11,021 --> 00:39:16,320
We were sitting in our seats and somebody shouted fire.

364
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:24,060
And then all of a sudden I saw a sheet of flame come about 70 miles an hour

365
00:39:24,061 --> 00:39:31,660
up from the doorway all the way across the room towards where I was.

366
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:36,820
The flash fire raced through the nightclub fueled by satin draperies,

367
00:39:36,821 --> 00:39:40,780
plastic upholstery and papier-mâché decorations.

368
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:46,020
The building was fireproof, but its contents were not.

369
00:39:46,021 --> 00:39:50,660
I couldn't see, but I could feel, and I felt my hands.

370
00:39:51,620 --> 00:39:56,440
And it felt like there was bacon on the back of my hands.

371
00:39:56,700 --> 00:40:01,220
It was all burned off and raised just like bacon.

372
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:07,720
And I said, oh my God, how can I possibly be a doctor?

373
00:40:07,800 --> 00:40:08,720
I was going to be a surgeon.

374
00:40:11,020 --> 00:40:14,540
Most patrons inside the club found it impossible to escape.

375
00:40:15,380 --> 00:40:20,360
There were no emergency lights, and the revolving door exits quickly clogged with bodies.

376
00:40:22,420 --> 00:40:29,200
People were actually piled up in front of the doors and caught in the revolving doors,

377
00:40:29,201 --> 00:40:34,300
so that eventually close to two-thirds of the people at the Coconut Grove died.

378
00:40:35,700 --> 00:40:40,400
In 12 minutes, 492 people were dead or dying.

379
00:40:41,020 --> 00:40:43,420
I saw scenes of indescribable horror.

380
00:40:44,280 --> 00:40:45,820
The street was in chaos.

381
00:40:46,620 --> 00:40:48,060
People were running around screaming.

382
00:40:48,380 --> 00:40:49,740
Bodies were lying on the ground.

383
00:40:50,520 --> 00:40:53,900
These people were all dead and all horribly burned.

384
00:40:55,380 --> 00:41:00,720
Hundreds of survivors were rushed to hospitals, where doctors were overwhelmed.

385
00:41:01,600 --> 00:41:04,660
Two of the residents came in and they looked at me and said,

386
00:41:04,740 --> 00:41:06,220
Oh, he's dead. Cover him up.

387
00:41:07,020 --> 00:41:08,900
And I said, No, I'm not. I'm alive.

388
00:41:08,901 --> 00:41:13,900
and I must have shaken them up a little bit, I think.

389
00:41:14,380 --> 00:41:16,700
So they began working on me.

390
00:41:17,640 --> 00:41:20,700
Otherwise they would have just let me go and I would have been dead.

391
00:41:21,560 --> 00:41:27,920
The horror of the Coconut Grove fire generated a renewed call for better fire safety.

392
00:41:28,960 --> 00:41:33,040
The Coconut Grove was something that shocked not only Bostonians,

393
00:41:33,180 --> 00:41:34,760
but people across the country.

394
00:41:34,761 --> 00:41:39,960
We saw these people being brought to morgues, that hundreds of people had died,

395
00:41:40,540 --> 00:41:43,740
and eventually thousands of families were affected.

396
00:41:44,260 --> 00:41:45,240
It could have been anyone.

397
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:51,120
Reforms that followed the fire included restrictions on the use of flammable decorations

398
00:41:51,121 --> 00:41:56,880
and requirements for improved fire exits in public buildings.

399
00:41:58,780 --> 00:42:03,080
The Triangle and Coconut Grove incidents were vivid reminders

400
00:42:03,081 --> 00:42:09,240
that despite our best efforts, fire can strike without warning, leaving tragedy in its wake.

401
00:42:22,820 --> 00:42:27,720
America's fire departments are some of the best equipped and best trained units in the world.

402
00:42:27,721 --> 00:42:35,280
modern pumpers can shoot more than 9,000 gallons of water per minute

403
00:42:35,281 --> 00:42:43,180
ladders and snorkel rigs can now reach the 24th floor of a burning building

404
00:42:43,181 --> 00:42:49,820
however firefighting remains one of the deadliest occupations in america

405
00:42:49,821 --> 00:42:56,560
nearly 100 firefighters die every year in the line of duty

406
00:42:57,520 --> 00:43:01,700
More than 90,000 firefighters are injured every year.

407
00:43:03,460 --> 00:43:07,500
Regardless of all of this equipment you've got, it still comes down to the firefighter

408
00:43:07,501 --> 00:43:11,080
and him having nerve enough and guts enough to get in there,

409
00:43:11,081 --> 00:43:17,100
and it's always going to be the unknown that will get you in the end.

410
00:43:19,660 --> 00:43:23,540
Scientists continue to develop better firefighting tools,

411
00:43:23,541 --> 00:43:26,220
such as improved heat-resistant clothing.

412
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:32,880
And simple things like smoke detectors have dramatically reduced fire fatalities.

413
00:43:35,440 --> 00:43:39,560
Engineers are also developing household sprinkler systems

414
00:43:39,561 --> 00:43:43,340
to add another level of protection inside the home.

415
00:43:45,120 --> 00:43:50,140
It's uncertain, though, that the next generation of technology can conquer

416
00:43:50,141 --> 00:43:53,100
nature's most frightening force.

417
00:43:53,540 --> 00:43:56,320
Fires are going to break out.

418
00:43:57,020 --> 00:44:01,600
There's nothing in the foreseeable future where we are ever going to conquer fire.

419
00:44:01,940 --> 00:44:04,260
There are better tools for the firefighter to use.

420
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:05,340
They're better protected.

421
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:10,200
There are building codes and fire prevention regulations that are available

422
00:44:10,201 --> 00:44:13,540
that will help reduce and stop fires from occurring,

423
00:44:13,780 --> 00:44:16,360
but there are always going to be fires starting.

424
00:44:16,361 --> 00:44:25,220
I'm John Travolta from the new movie Ladder 49.

425
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:30,340
Thanks for watching Modern Marvels, the history of firefighting on the History Channel.

426
00:44:32,480 --> 00:44:37,640
Tonight on Modern Marvels, could you imagine watching the big game on this screen?

427
00:44:37,840 --> 00:44:40,000
It's close to five football fields long.

428
00:44:40,180 --> 00:44:43,060
There's over 12 million lights on the canopy.

429
00:44:43,480 --> 00:44:45,500
Just pray one of those lights doesn't go out.

430
00:44:45,501 --> 00:44:51,820
Get ready for more of the world's biggest machines on Modern Marvels, tonight at 10 on the History Channel.

